事情。而且，你知道的，其他各种态度，我现在会说这些态度有点太负面、太极端了。直到多年后，我逐渐真正结识了许多现实生活中的超心理学家。我意识到，你知道的，这其实并不是真的。在这里，我可以特别提到的一个人是已故的罗伯特·莫里斯教授（Professor Robert Morris），即爱丁堡大学的库斯勒超心理学讲座教授（Kresge Chair，译者注：原文为Kursler，实际为Kresg Chair或Koestler Chair，这里按发音），以及他的门生、现任库斯勒讲座教授卡罗琳·瓦特（Caroline Watt），她是我非常好的朋友。他们两人——当然鲍勃肯定是，卡罗琳可能稍微少一点，但鲍勃绝对比起怀疑论者更像是一个信徒，但他非常愿意倾听……

46
00:26:17.000 --> 00:26:55.000
skeptics had to say. I really like that kind of open-mindedness. And also, he had kind of, there were two strands to the work that was looking to see whether maybe you, be things like telepathy, precognition, etc., etc., really did exist. That was one strand, and that's probably what the major emphasis of that unit was about. But also a second strand, looking at why people might sometimes think they've had a paranormal experience when, in fact, they hadn't. I mean, his first PhD student was my good friend, Richard Wiseman, who, you know, is one of the kind of leading skeptics in terms of all this stuff as well.
怀疑论者要说的话。我真的很喜欢那种思想开放的态度。而且，他的工作有两条主线：一条是寻找诸如心灵感应、预知等现象是否真的存在。这是一条主线，这可能是该研究单位的主要重点。但还有第二条主线，即探讨为什么人们有时会认为自己有了超自然体验，而实际上并没有。我的意思是，他的第一个博士研究生是我的好朋友理查德·怀斯曼（Richard Wiseman），你知道的，他也是在所有这些事情上处于领先地位的怀疑论者之一。

47
00:26:55.000 --> 00:27:47.000
And so, as I say, for many years, in my lectures, I would talk about pseudoscience generally, but when I talked about parapsychology, it was as pseudoscience, as I then saw it. And then I came across a paper where somebody had put this to the test empirically by looking at the typical kind of criticisms in terms of the criteria for pseudoscience that skeptics direct to parapsychology. and it's basically said, well, you know, are they true? By looking at three journals from kind of mainstream science, including the British Journal of Psychology, three journals from fringe sciences, including the Journal of Parasicology, and really on that basis, it didn't look like these were fair criticisms, the vast majority of them.
所以正如我所说，多年来在我的讲座中，我通常会谈论伪科学，但当我谈到超心理学时，我把它当作一门伪科学来谈，正如我当时所看到的那样。然后我偶然看到了一篇论文，有人通过观察怀疑论者针对超心理学的伪科学标准所提出的典型批评，对其进行了实证检验。它基本上是在说，好吧，你知道的，这些批评是真的吗？通过查阅来自主流科学的三本期刊（包括《英国心理学杂志》）以及来自边缘科学的三本期刊（包括《超心理学杂志》），实际上在此基础上，这些批评看起来并不公平，绝大多数都是如此。

48
00:27:47.000 --> 00:28:31.000
And so on that basis, and ultimately, science is a method rather than a body of established facts. And you can look at any issue either scientifically or non-scientifically, but if you're looking at an issue scientifically, using methodological controls and statistical analysis and so on and so forth, why would you not say that's a science? And so, again, one final argument, people like myself, Richard Wiseman, James Randy, a whole host of other skeptics have sometimes directly tested paranormal claims, in which case we're doing parapsychology, and I would hope We're doing it scientifically. So parapsychology, at its best, I think, has to be described as a science.
因此，基于这一点，归根结底，科学是一种方法，而不是一组既定事实的事实集合。你可以用科学或非科学的方式来看待任何问题，但如果你正在用科学的方式看待一个问题，使用方法论控制和统计分析等等，你为什么不可以说这是一门科学呢？所以，还有一个最终的论点，像我本人、理查德·怀斯曼、詹姆斯·兰迪以及其他许多怀疑论者，有时会直接测试超自然的声称，在这种情况下我们就是在做超心理学，我希望我们是以科学的方式在做。因此，超心理学在其最佳状态下，我认为必须被描述为一门科学。

49
00:28:31.000 --> 00:29:10.000
This is not a popular view among skeptics, I hasten to it. Our skeptics would disagree, but that's my position now. But essentially, I mean, if you design a study scientifically, you know, you could potentially potentially study anything, I mean, as long as you're following the prescribed method. But, you know, that raises another question in my mind. mind, which is that there have been studies that have been published in esteemed journals, peer-reviewed journals about parapsychology, and some of them have indicated an existence, and then some of them have been retracted. So, you know, why is that happening? Is that,
我得赶紧补充一句，这在怀疑论者当中并不是一个受欢迎的观点。其他怀疑论者会有所不同，但这就是我现在的立场。但本质上，我的意思是，如果你以科学的方式设计一项研究，你知道的，你几乎可以研究任何东西，我的意思是，只要你遵循规定的方法。但是，你知道的，这在我的脑海中引发了另一个问题，那就是有些研究已经发表在受人尊敬的期刊、同行评审期刊上，关于超心理学，其中一些研究表明其存在，然后其中一些又被撤回了。所以，你知道的，为什么会这样？是因为，

50
00:29:10.000 --> 00:29:46.000
is that because we're not skeptical enough? I think it's, I mean, it is one of those, obviously, hugely controversial areas, and people, you know, emotions are high on both sides. I mean, one of the most recent episodes would be kind of Darrell Bem's controversial paper on precognition. And that was published in, I think it was 2011. Again, without going into all the details, Bem, who has obviously been around for many, many decades, very respected figure within psychology, but also unusually for a psychologist,
是因为我们不够怀疑吗？我认为这是——我的意思是，这显然是那些极具争议的领域之一，人们情绪高涨，双方皆是如此。我的意思是，最近的事件之一将是达尔·贝姆（Darrell Bem）关于预知的颇具争议的论文。那发表在，我想大概是2011年。同样，不深入所有细节，贝姆在心理学界已经活跃了数十年，是一个非常受人尊敬的人物，但对于心理学家来说很不寻常的是，

51
00:29:46.000 --> 00:30:18.000
known to be very sympathetic towards parapsychology and paranormal claims. And he had already done this before in, With respect to the Gansfeld studies, rather than publishing in Parasicology journals, where the kind of mainstream science media wouldn't even bother to read the stuff, he'd made a point of trying to publish in really the top journals in psychology and succeeded on at least two occasions. And it's kind of interesting when somebody does that, because it raises all kinds of issues.
众所周知，他对超心理学和超自然声称非常同情。而且他以前在甘斯菲尔德研究方面就已经这样做过了，比起在超心理学期刊上发表（主流科学媒体甚至懒得去读那些东西），他特意试图在心理学顶级期刊上发表，并且在至少两次机会中成功了。当有人这样做时，这就变得很有趣，因为它引发了各种各样的问题。

52
00:30:18.000 --> 00:30:52.000
just to kind of summarize, he published in the more recent study, the studies of precognition. There were nine experiments, over 1,000 participants in total. And basically, he was arguing that he had found pretty compelling evidence for the reality of people being able to sense future events before they happen pre-cognition. One of the very good things that he did, this paper I hasten to add, had been through, I think it was half a dozen different referees. It had done to the standard refereeing process. And at the end of the day,
简单总结一下，他在那项更新的研究中发表了关于预知的研究。总共有九个实验，超过一千名参与者。基本上，他认为自己找到了相当令人信服的证据，证明人们能够感知未来事件——即预知。他做得很好的一点是，这篇论文，我急需补充的是，经过了，我想大概有六个不同的审稿人的审阅。它完成了标准的审稿流程。最后，

53
00:30:52.000 --> 00:31:21.000
the editor had felt they had no choice other than to publish, even though he probably knew that he was going to be a very controversial paper. One of the good things about what Ben did was to say he wanted other people to try and replicate the effects, and he would make his software available. We decided to chase up on that. we being in this case Richard Wiseman and Stuart Ritchie. I must admit we had a slightly ulterior motive here.
编辑觉得除了发表之外别无选择，尽管他可能知道这是一篇极具争议的论文。本所做的好事之一就是表示他希望其他人尝试复制这些实验效果，并且他会公开他的软件。我们决定跟进这件事。我们在这个案例中是指理查德·威斯曼（Richard Wiseman）和斯图尔特·里奇（Stuart Ritchie）。我必须承认，我们当时其实有点别的动机。

54
00:31:21.000 --> 00:31:45.000
We didn't really expect to replicate the effects, but we did think this might be a relatively easy way to get a paper in a top journal. That was our hope. But anyway, it still took a lot of work. We each did an independent replication of Bem's experiment number nine. I can go with the details of what the experiment involved do you like, but just for the time being, I'll talk about the fact we didn't get the same
我们其实并没有期望复制出效果，但我们确实认为这可能是在顶级期刊上发表论文的一种相对简便的方法。那是我们的期望。不过，这仍然需要大量的工作。我们各自对本的第九个实验进行了独立的重复。如果你想听的话，我可以详细讲讲实验的内容，但目前为止，我先说说我们没有得到相同结果的事实。

55
00:31:45.000 --> 00:32:30.000
results as Bem. We weren't surprised. We wrote it up. We sent it into the journal of personality and social psychology, and it was rejected without being sent out for peer review. And that's what really annoyed us, because this paper, Ben's paper had had huge coverage right around the world. So it's important whether these effects are real or not, and we had failed to replicate. But the attitude was, no, no, no, we don't publish replications. We were a bit cross about that, but we tried two other high-impact journals and got the same treatment. And again, we weren't saying, you know, you must publish this. We were saying, send it out for peer review. The British Journal of Psychology did send it out for peer review.
结果与本不同。我们并不感到意外。我们把报告写了出来，投给了《人格与社会心理学杂志》，结果在没有送去同行评议的情况下就被拒稿了。这正是让我们最恼火的地方，因为这篇论文，本的论文在全世界引起了巨大的报道。所以这些效应是否真实存在非常重要，而我们未能复制出来。但他们的态度是，不，不，不，我们不发表复制性研究。我们对此有点生气，但我们又尝试了其他两家高影响力期刊，受到了同样的对待。再说一次，我们并不是说，你必须发表这个。我们说的是，把它送去同行评议吧。《英国心理学杂志》确实把它送去同行评议了。

56
00:32:30.000 --> 00:33:11.000
One referee really loved the paper. The other one had concerns. It wasn't too keen. And we could recognized from what the concerns that Rexrest were, we thought, we think we know who this referee is. We think it's a certain Darrell Bem. We asked him and he confirmed it was him. So clearly it's a conflict of interest there. How is that even possible that the editor would use him as a referee? That's what happened. So again, we said, well, you know, sent out to a third reviewer. Nope. Long story short, we published it in plus one. which, as you'll know, is an open access journal, which turned out to be a good move
一位审稿人非常喜欢这篇论文。另一位则有顾虑，不太热心。从这位审稿人提出的顾虑中，我们认出来了，心想，我们大概知道这位审稿人是谁了。我们觉得这人就是达里尔·本（Darrell Bem）。我们去问了他，他确认就是他自己。显然，这其中存在利益冲突。编辑怎么可能用他作为审稿人呢？事情就是这样。所以我们又说，好吧，能不能送给第三位审稿人？不行。简而言之，我们把它发表在了PLOS ONE上。你知道，这是一个开放获取期刊，结果证明这是一个明智的举动，

57
00:33:11.000 --> 00:33:37.000
because at one point we were getting like a thousand views a day, which is amazing for a scientific paper. And it also fed into the ongoing discussions and debate about the replication crisis in psychology. Because this was a situation where I say the paper had gone through, the normal process of being reviewed and so on and so forth, and it had been published. Now, some people took the application,
因为一度我们每天能获得大约一千次浏览，这对于一篇科学论文来说相当惊人。这也融入了当时关于心理学复制危机的持续讨论和辩论中。因为这是一种情况，就像我说的，论文经历了正常的评审等流程，并且已经发表了。现在，有些人采取的看法是，

58
00:33:37.000 --> 00:34:28.000
of, well, it can't possibly, the results can't possibly be true, therefore, how could this happen? There's something wrong with the whole process, the whole kind of scientific publication. Others were kind of more open-minded about whether possibly the results might be true or not. I think what it did, it made me think long and hard about the ways in which spurious results may end up being published and the kind of pressures that are on. And now, you know, the debate has kind of advanced to the extent that we all recognize that what we might call questionable research practice is not out and out fraud, much more subtle than that, but just kind of giving yourself the benefit of the doubt, knowing that you've got to get an effect that's just below that 0.05 level
嗯，这绝对不可能，结果绝不可能是真的，因此，怎么会发生这种事？整个流程，整个科学出版体系都有问题。其他人则对结果是否可能真实持更加开放的态度。我认为这件事让我深刻反思了虚假结果最终如何得以发表的方式，以及身上所承受的压力。现在，你知道，辩论已经进展到我们都承认的地步，我们所谓的有问题的研究实践并非彻头彻尾的欺诈，比那要微妙得多，只是某种程度上给自己留有余地，明知你需要得到一个刚好低于0.05显著性水平的效果，

59
00:34:28.000 --> 00:35:04.000
of significance, otherwise the editors won't look at it. Then it kind of really highlighted how those issues can basically distort what ends up being published. And maybe particularly in what we would think of as the top journals, because they have a bias towards not only saying, well, we don't publish replications because they're not interesting enough, we don't publish negative findings. And, you know, so all the negative findings and all the kind of just boring old replications just don't see, ever see the light of day. Or usually in this case, as I say, we actually managed to kind of get it published.
否则编辑就不会看它。这就真正凸显了这些问题是如何从根本上扭曲最终发表的内容的。也许特别是在我们认为的顶级期刊中，因为它们有一种偏见，不仅体现在说，嗯，我们不发表复制性研究，因为它们不够有趣，我们也不发表否定性发现。然后，你知道，所有的否定性发现和所有那些无聊的旧复制研究就根本见不到天日。通常在这个案例中，正如我所说，我们实际上设法把它发表了。

60
00:35:04.000 --> 00:35:23.000
Although that may be changing. I mean, I think there's more of a tendency to... Oh, I think it definitely is, and I think it's a healthy thing. I think I'm kind of... People talk about the kind of crisis in psychology. I just see it as a kind of healthy period of self-criticism. I must admit, I feel slightly guilty about it, because I can look back now and see papers that I have published in the past
尽管情况可能会改变。我的意思是，我认为有一种趋势……哦，我想它绝对是这样的，而且我认为这是一种健康的现象。我想我有点……人们谈论心理学领域的所谓危机。我只把它看作是一个健康的自我批评时期。我必须承认，我对此感到有些内疚，因为我现在回过头去，可以看到我过去发表的论文，

61
00:35:23.000 --> 00:36:00.000
and think that probably wasn't a real effect. You know, things that I published and then tried to replicate myself and failed. And, you know, if you even try to send off a paper that's about these failed replications, even though it's your own work, you know, you're trying to say, no, I don't think that was real, after all, you can't get it published. Whereas now, I think, as you say, things do seem to be in the direction where that's going to be easier to do. So, yeah, so it's a bit, it's been a good outcome there, I think. So I know you do a lot of writing for the general public about the work that you do.
并认为那可能并不是一种真实的效应。你知道，那些我发表了后来自己试图复制却失败了的事情。而且，你知道，如果你甚至尝试去发表一篇关于这些失败复制的论文，哪怕那是你自己的工作，你知道，你想说，不，我终究认为那不是真的，你是无法发表它的。而现在，我想正如你所说，事情似乎正朝着更容易做到的方向发展。所以，是的，所以这是一个好结果，我想。所以我知道你为大众写了很多关于你所做的工作的文章。

62
00:36:00.000 --> 00:37:12.000
And what is one lesson that you'd want people to take away from your research? I mean, I think what I just heard was part of it that science is evolving and that people should understand that we all need to keep an open mind. But, I mean, what would you say to just our average listener about what they should be thinking, say, about the paranormal, whether they believe in ghosts or think they've been inducted by aliens? What would your best advice be? I suppose, if maybe allow me kind of two or three, a little nuggets here rather than just one. I mean, one thing would be that I think it's best to base your beliefs about the way the universe operates on kind of solid empirical evidence, preferably from well-controlled studies, rather than personal experience. Now, for most people, personal experience is the gold standard. Even still in the legal system, eyewitness testimony is given far more credence than it should be, given what we know about the way memory works.
你希望人们从你的研究中汲取的一个教训是什么？我的意思是，我想我刚刚听到的一部分是科学在不断发展，人们应该明白我们都需要保持开放的心态。但是，我的意思是，对于我们的普通听众，关于他们应该如何看待超自然现象，无论是相信鬼魂还是认为自己被外星人绑架，你会对他们说些什么？你最好的建议是什么？我想，如果允许我提出两三个小建议，而不是只有一个。我的意思是，第一点是，我认为最好将你对宇宙运作方式的信仰建立在坚实的经验证据之上，最好是来自控制良好的研究，而不是个人经验。现在，对于大多数人来说，个人经验是金标准。甚至在法律系统中，目击者证词受到的信任也远超其应得水平，考虑到我们对记忆运作方式的了解。

63
00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:59.000
So, you know, I think that would be one piece of advice that I think I'd like to pass on. And I think the other thing is kind of as you've hinted out there, that science is not. not a kind of an established body of facts. It's something that evolves. And scientists will sometimes change their minds on issues. And that's a good thing. You know, it's, it's, it's a pity we don't have kind of more politicians who are able to do that. But because of the way political systems work, it's political suicide to change your mind on an issue. I mean, that's how we've ended up with Brexit and maybe how you ended up with Trump. But let's not go.
所以，你知道，我想这将是我愿意传达的一个建议。我想另一件事就像你刚才暗示的那样，科学不是一种既定事实的集合。它是一个不断发展的事物。科学家有时会改变他们对问题的看法。这是一件好事。你知道，遗憾的是，我们没有更多能够做到这一点的政客。但由于政治系统的运作方式，在某个问题上改变看法是政治自杀。我的意思是，这就是我们如何最终导致脱欧，也许也是你如何导致特朗普上台的。但是我们不要去谈这个。

64
00:37:59.000 --> 00:38:23.000
But, you know, I mean, there's, I actually respect people greatly who kind of stand up and say, actually, I've realized I was wrong about that. I think it's a good thing to do. Whereas, you know, there is still this tendency for people to see that as being kind of wishy-washy and you can't make your mind up. I think that's a mistake. Well, this has been a fascinating exchange. I really appreciate you joining us today, Dr. French.
但是，你知道，我的意思是，实际上我很尊敬那些站出来说，事实上，我意识到我在那件事上弄错了的人。我认为这是一件好事。然而，你知道，人们仍然有一种倾向，认为那是优柔寡断，是你无法下定决心。我认为这是一个错误。嗯，这是一次迷人的交流。非常感谢你今天加入我们，法国博士。

